  ],
  "text": "Pluto never moved, never shrank, and still stopped being a planet. For seventy six years it was the ninth dot on every classroom poster. Then astronomers found Eris, further out than Pluto and slightly heavier. So either we had a tenth planet, or the word planet meant nothing. It had never actually been defined. So in two thousand six, astronomers finally wrote the rules. Orbit the Sun. Be round. Sweep your own lane clean. Pluto passes the first two easily. But its orbit is crowded with thousands of icy chunks it never cleared. So a few hundred astronomers in a hall in Prague raised their hands. Pluto did not change that day. Our definition did. And every planet still on that list is stranger than the one we removed.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro"
}
